    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/tc-12-2159-2018",
    "abstract": "Abstract. The surface radiation budget of the Arctic Ocean plays a central role in summer ice melt and is governed by clouds and surface albedo. I calculated the net radiation flux for a range of albedos under sunny and cloudy skies and determined the break-even value, where the net radiation is the same for cloudy and sunny skies. Break-even albedos range from 0.30 in September to 0.58 in July. For snow-covered or bare ice, sunny skies always result in less radiative heat input. In contrast, leads always have, and ponds usually have, more radiative input under sunny skies than cloudy skies. Snow-covered ice has a net radiation flux that is negative or near zero under sunny skies, resulting in radiative cooling. Areally averaged albedos for sea ice in July result in a smaller net radiation flux under cloudy skies. For May, June, August, and September, the net radiation is smaller under sunny skies.",
    "field": "Arctic sea ice-atmosphere interactions",
    "problem": "The challenge is quantifying the net radiative impact of Arctic summer clouds, as their cooling \"umbrella\" effect and warming \"blanket\" effect depend heavily on the variable surface albedo, making it difficult to predict their ultimate influence on sea ice melt.",
    "method": "The author calculates net radiation flux for various albedos using paired sunny and cloudy day observations from the SHEBA campaign, determining the break-even albedo where net radiation is equal under both sky conditions to assess the radiative impact of clouds on different Arctic surfaces.",
    "task": "This paper's task is to determine whether sunny or cloudy skies result in more radiative warming of the Arctic sea ice by calculating the net radiation flux for a range of surface albedos and identifying the \"break-even\" albedo where the net radiation is equal.",
